WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.100
Hi, I'm Simon Sinek

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.700
and this is Adrian Zackheim

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:08.000
and we are the co-publishers of a new imprint at Penguin Random House called

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.600
Optimism Press.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Adrian has been a publisher

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.942
at Penguin Random House

00:00:13.942 --> 00:00:15.300
for how many years?

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.500
Seventeen years.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.100
Seventeen years.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.357
Longer than I've been alive.

00:00:20.357 --> 00:00:24.184
I've been an author with Penguin Random House

00:00:24.184 --> 00:00:26.300
for more than a decade now

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:31.470
so this is something that has
been a long time in the making

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.500
where we decided to start something new
together.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.500
So we called this imprint that we've started

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.000
Optimism Press.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.600
What is the reason
that you wanted to call it Optimism Press?

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:46.000
Well since this is a mission

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.800
that you wanted to undertake in the beginning

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:49.552
and since

00:00:49.552 --> 00:00:52.560
you identify yourself as an optimist

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.938
and optimism is clearly part of your mission

00:00:55.938 --> 00:00:57.780
and everything you do

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.569
it just seemed like 
the natural jumping-off point

00:01:00.569 --> 00:01:02.220
for anything we were gonna do together

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.080
that really was under your brand.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.900
So it wasn't a difficult question for me

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.840
and I don't think it was for you either.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.200
No.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.000
I think it's a perfect shelf.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.100
Optimism is a perfect shelf

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.600
for which all ideas that

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.300
either mine 
or ones that I fall in love with

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.600
should sit on.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.860
They should be first and foremost
forward-facing

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:34.600
and have a confidence that 
the future is bright.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:39.260
And I think all of the ideas 
that we hope to share with the world

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.400
will pay off 
the promise of optimism.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.756
What is your vision for Optimism Press?

00:01:44.756 --> 00:01:46.649
I imagine a world

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:47.812
that does not yet exist.

00:01:47.812 --> 00:01:49.030
I imagine a world in which

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:51.961
the vast majority of people
wake up every single morning inspired

00:01:51.961 --> 00:01:53.400
feel safe at work

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.000
and return home fulfilled at the end of the day.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.480
I've completely devoted my life and career

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.417
to trying to build this world

00:02:00.417 --> 00:02:04.100
and one of the nice things about my journey
is that

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.300
I have the opportunity to meet some wonderful people

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.900
who are contributing to building that world.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.253
They're running organizations,

00:02:09.253 --> 00:02:11.006
they're inside organizations,

00:02:11.006 --> 00:02:11.978
they are leaders,

00:02:11.978 --> 00:02:14.039
they're all sorts of people.

00:02:14.039 --> 00:02:15.500
And some of them have ideas

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.540
that I believe are so significant

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.900
that the rest of us 
need to know those ideas.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:20.707
And so for me

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:23.500
it was so exciting to have the opportunity

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.213
to form my own imprint with
Penguin Random House

00:02:26.213 --> 00:02:27.909
that we have an opportunity

00:02:27.909 --> 00:02:31.100
to introduce the rest of the world to 
the people and the ideas

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:34.100
that I think are essential pieces of the jigsaw puzzle

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.701
to help build that vision.

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:36.823
And what gave you the idea

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:39.300
to work with Penguin Random House
in the first place?

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.000
Well you and I ...

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.722
You've been my publisher for all my books

00:02:41.722 --> 00:02:43.920
and you and I have known each other for

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:44.900
over 10 years now

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.020
over a decade.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.900
And I think it's safe to say that 
we do good work together.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.140
I could not have written any of my books
without you.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:56.300
And it's become more than a business relationship

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:57.900
it's a true friendship

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.862
and a true collaboration.

00:02:59.862 --> 00:03:02.019
And I trust you immensely

00:03:02.019 --> 00:03:03.400
and I trust your judgement

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.682
and you are extremely good at
taking good ideas

00:03:06.682 --> 00:03:08.600
and making them into great ideas.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.720
And so when when I had this idea
to form the imprint

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.200
there's quite frankly no one else I'd rather partner with

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:15.900
to help do it

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.000
because we will find good ideas

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.500
and we'll make them into great books.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:20.125
And in fact

00:03:20.125 --> 00:03:23.394
there were books before the imprint
that we collaborated on.

00:03:23.394 --> 00:03:24.200
Not your books

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.139
but other writers.

00:03:25.139 --> 00:03:26.900
Many of which were quite successful

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.919
and we had fun doing it.

00:03:29.919 --> 00:03:31.400
It's true.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.200
We have helped

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.359
we have done things together

00:03:34.359 --> 00:03:36.173
under different imprints and it worked out well

00:03:36.173 --> 00:03:37.790
so it was a good
we had precedence.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.430
This was low risk for you and me
as we knew what we could do.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.220
So what was the reason
you wanted to do an imprint with me?

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.200
Well it goes back to the same question

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.780
we had worked together on books

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:48.980
that you had found in your travels

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:52.140
and several of them had worked out really well

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.600
and it introduced me to some fantastic
writers and thinkers.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.300
And so when you said

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.900
why don't we formulate a strategy together

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:02.859
and do this in a sort of planned way

00:04:02.859 --> 00:04:05.562
instead of just by lucky accident

00:04:05.562 --> 00:04:07.540
I jumped at the opportunity.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:12.519
It seemed like like a fantastic chance to
do some great publishing.

00:04:12.519 --> 00:04:16.000
I think one of the things that
people don't realize about our friendship

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.500
is that a lot of the risks that we take
aren't actually risks for us.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.800
I think you and I see a lot of things we do
as no-brainers.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.900
We have common cause

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.080
and we know how to work together.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:27.267
And I think other people

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:28.800
when we say we're gonna do this

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:29.711
people go (gasp).

00:04:29.711 --> 00:04:30.300
But we go

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.657
no, no, it'll be fine.

00:04:32.657 --> 00:04:35.500
And it's worked out okay.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:36.800
Knock on wood.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:37.760
So far so good.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.600
It's been fun.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.340
Simon,
what what do you hope to achieve

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.200
with the new imprint?

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.990
I know that my ideas are a piece of a
bigger jigsaw puzzle

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:47.600
my own books.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.409
This big vision that I have

00:04:50.409 --> 00:04:53.800
I have a couple of the pieces.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.300
And so what I'm hoping is that
these books

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.000
as we build them over the course of time

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.680
offer a road, a path,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.104
a compass direction

00:05:04.104 --> 00:05:07.721
on how we actually build this world
that's different from now.

00:05:07.721 --> 00:05:09.000
The goal is to give

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.400
not only to share the ideas that
we stumble upon

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.400
that we think are valuable

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.680
but also to give bigger
platforms to our authors.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:20.489
I hope that they all have bigger and
more successful careers

00:05:20.489 --> 00:05:22.330
as speakers for this movement

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:24.460
for their own ideas.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:27.370
So I'm really hoping to give them a bigger, louder platform

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:31.017
because certainly all the authors we've
signed up so far

00:05:31.017 --> 00:05:32.900
we've fallen in love with them and their ideas

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:37.100
and we want others to fall in love with
them and their ideas too.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.300
Tell us about some of the authors that
we've already signed up.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.385
So the book that we are launching with is

00:05:44.385 --> 00:05:47.200
by Summer Rayne Oakes

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:48.700
It's a book called

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.102
"How To Make A Plant Love You."

00:05:50.102 --> 00:05:51.909
I met Summer many, many years ago.

00:05:51.909 --> 00:05:55.160
We've been close friends for awhile.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.900
She's just a powerhouse

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:58.900
and an optimist

00:05:58.900 --> 00:05:59.900
and a wonderful human being.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:05.000
And she has this fantastic idea about
how to raise plants

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.200
how to look after plants.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.720
She's been featured in the press multiple
times because

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:09.820
she has I don't remember

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:10.620
one hundred

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.235
six hundred plants in her
New York City apartment

00:06:12.235 --> 00:06:13.269
in her Brooklyn apartment

00:06:13.269 --> 00:06:13.815
and I've been

00:06:13.815 --> 00:06:15.547
I think it's up to seven, 
was up to seven

00:06:15.547 --> 00:06:17.850
and it's an amazing place to visit

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:20.840
sort of move the vines out of the way to wash the dishes.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:23.349
It's really fantastic.

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:30.070
And she these plants grow and thrive
inside her New York City apartment and

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:30.950
it's because

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:31.867
it's not by luck

00:06:31.867 --> 00:06:34.330
it's because she understands the plants and

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.849
she understands where to put them and
how to nurture them.

00:06:36.849 --> 00:06:38.961
And so the way we treat plants is

00:06:38.961 --> 00:06:41.649
we see a plant that we think is pretty and

00:06:41.649 --> 00:06:42.149
we think 
ooh it would look nice over there on that coffee table

00:06:42.149 --> 00:06:44.620
and we buy the plant

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:46.052
we put on the coffee table

00:06:46.052 --> 00:06:47.098
and the plant dies.

00:06:47.098 --> 00:06:48.320
It's because we bought the plant that we wanted

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.751
and put it where we wanted it.

00:06:50.751 --> 00:06:52.220
And the reality is 
you have to know where the plant wants
to be

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:55.659
not where we want to put it

00:06:55.659 --> 00:06:56.159
and you have to put it in the place where
it's more likely to thrive.

00:06:56.159 --> 00:06:58.977
What I love about Summer's work is

00:06:58.977 --> 00:07:00.220
it's a metaphor

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:02.220
because we do that with people.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:03.342
We take somebody and

00:07:03.342 --> 00:07:05.964
we put them in the job that
we want them to do

00:07:05.964 --> 00:07:06.820
and then we get frustrated that they don't
succeed and

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:09.740
so we move them
and they don't succeed again

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.463
but we never actually think to ask

00:07:11.463 --> 00:07:13.389
where would they naturally thrive

00:07:13.389 --> 00:07:13.889
what environment, what position, what job
should we put that person

00:07:13.889 --> 00:07:16.556
and so that they'll do well.

00:07:16.556 --> 00:07:21.700
Though yes and on one level

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:22.924
Summer's book is actually a wonderful book

00:07:22.924 --> 00:07:24.759
for people who want to know more about how to look after plants

00:07:24.759 --> 00:07:26.985
but there's really a very important

00:07:26.985 --> 00:07:29.540
and deep-seated message contained within this book

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:30.831
about how to treat people.

00:07:30.831 --> 00:07:32.480
It's a really it's a big metaphor that if you learn how to take care of
plants

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:35.589
how to make a plant love you

00:07:35.589 --> 00:07:37.326
we're actually learning how to make
people love you

00:07:37.326 --> 00:07:40.558
how to make people fall in love with their jobs and each other.

00:07:40.558 --> 00:07:42.189
It's just it's just a wonderful

00:07:42.189 --> 00:07:46.459
and it's so full of depth
and she's such a talented writer.

00:07:46.459 --> 00:07:47.980
I couldn't think of a better book

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:51.879
that I'd rather introduce the world to our imprint with.

00:07:51.879 --> 00:07:55.520
We're also publishing a book by Bruce Deel

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:58.100
called "Trust First."

00:07:58.100 --> 00:07:58.600
As you know,
Bruce runs an organization called City
of Refuge in Atlanta, Georgia

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:04.880
and he

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:06.873
I don't know how to put it

00:08:06.873 --> 00:08:09.377
he saves people
to put it mildly

00:08:09.377 --> 00:08:10.640
drug addicts and hookers and
all these people

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.640
and he takes on the street
and he gives them a second chance

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:15.528
and he believes in them.

00:08:15.528 --> 00:08:17.887
And when you and I met Bruce

00:08:17.887 --> 00:08:19.000
I think we were

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.120
the whole room was enamored with him

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:23.080
because Bruce does something that I think few of us have the courage to do

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:27.440
which he gives people extreme trust.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:28.947
He takes somebody

00:08:28.947 --> 00:08:32.140
who the rest of society would say is unworthy of our trust

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:33.632
and he trusts them

00:08:33.632 --> 00:08:36.699
as if they were old friends or family.

00:08:36.699 --> 00:08:38.654
And what he has learned is that

00:08:38.654 --> 00:08:41.680
when you
take the risk to trust people they rise

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:47.529
to the occasion and they they're able to
transform their lives they're able to

00:08:47.529 --> 00:08:51.040
transform their path
and to one that is more positive for

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:56.380
them and those around them and though it
is a remarkable story about trust and

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:01.029
what Bruce is doing I the the it's an
extreme version of the lesson I think we

00:09:01.029 --> 00:09:06.279
could all learn I think few people and I
don't think we would ever recommend for

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:09.760
anybody to trust the way Bruce trusts I
think that that's a whole or another

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:15.130
order of trust but if we could learn to
trust better than we do now to take the

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:21.520
risk to trust people more than we do to
learn from Bruce's example I think it

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:24.730
makes for better organizations and it
makes for better collaboration you know

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:29.200
better work environments better cultures
better society better finish ups talk

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:34.300
about inspiring when I brought Bruce and
summer to your attention

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:40.120
what's the reason that you also said but
we have to do these books well you know

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:45.370
I examined our earliest conversations
about this program which was to give to

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:51.130
seize an opportunity to identify voices
that were inspiring in some way that

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:57.760
inspired us to to operate on a higher
level in the case of summer yes as you

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:02.380
said it's about plants but it's really
about living life at a higher level and

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:09.040
an enhancing life so that was an easy
one and then Bruce not only does he

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:13.690
trust people but in his case he put
himself and his family and not

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:20.290
inconsiderable risk from for many years
and became a living example of how Trust

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:26.740
transforms individuals families
communities and in the case of his

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:32.950
hometown Atlanta whole cities and he has
been a tremendous change agent so on

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:39.279
every level that book and and and he
himself personally his is a source of

00:10:39.279 --> 00:10:43.570
huge inspiration and I think will change
lives I tell you what I'm excited about

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:46.930
his imprint which a pattern is starting
to form in the books that were

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:51.100
publishing and planning to publish
though many books are about the idea

